%S A397621 1,1,1,1,2,2,1,1,3,2,2,2,2,3,1,1,4,3,3,3,2,2,3,2,3,3,2,3,3,4,1,1,5,4,
%T A397621 4,3,3,3,3,3,3,2,4,4,2,3,3,2,4,3,3,3,3,2,4,3,3,3,3,4,4,5,1,1,6,5,5,4,
%U A397621 4,4,4,4,3,4,3,3,4,3,4,3,4,4,3,5,2,4,4,4,4,5,2,3,4,4,3,2,5,4,4,4,3,3,4,4,3
%N A397621 a(n) is the linear complexity over GF(2) of the binary expansion of n (as computed by the Berlekamp-Massey algorithm).
%C A397621 a(n) is the degree of the minimal connection polynomial over GF(2) satisfied by the binary digit sequence of n (most significant bit first); equivalently, the length of the shortest linear feedback shift register (LFSR) that generates bin(n).
%C A397621 a(2^k)=1 and a(2^k-1)=1 for all k >= 0, since these binary strings consist of an initial 1 followed entirely by identical bits (all 0's or all 1's).
%C A397621 a(2^k+1)=k for all k>=1. These are the first occurrences of each new record value of a(n).
%C A397621 The graph of a(n) has a self-similar sawtooth shape: on each interval 2^(k-1) <= n < 2^k, a(n) equals 1 at both endpoints and reaches its largest values near the left endpoint, with a(2^(k-1)+1)=k-1.
%C A397621 The only fixed point is a(1)=1, since a(n) <= A070939(n)-1 < n for all n >= 2. Consequently, iterating n -> a(n) always converges to 1, typically within 2 or 3 steps regardless of the size of n (e.g. a(a(a(999999)))=1).
%D A397621 J.-M. Dornstetter, "On the equivalence between Berlekamp's and Euclid's algorithms", IEEE Transactions on Information Theory, Vol. IT-33 (1987), pp. 428-431.
%D A397621 James L. Massey, "Shift-register synthesis and BCH decoding", IEEE Transactions on Information Theory, Vol. IT-15 (1969), pp. 122-127.
%H A397621 Dieter Jungnickel, <a href="https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Linear_complexity_of_a_sequence">Linear complexity of a sequence</a>, Encyclopedia of Mathematics.
%H A397621 Tovohery H. Randrianarisoa, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.10034">Coding Theory using Linear Complexity of Finite Sequences</a>, arXiv:1802.10034 [cs.IT], 2018.
%H A397621 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlekamp%E2%80%93Massey_algorithm">Berlekamp-Massey algorithm</a>.
%F A397621 a(2^k)=1 for all k>=0.
%F A397621 a(2^k-1)=1 for all k>=1.
%F A397621 a(2^k+1)=k for all k>=1.
%F A397621 a(n)<=A070939(n)-1 whenever A070939(n)>=3.
%F A397621 Conjecture: a(A001317(n)) = A080079(n).
%e A397621 a(7) = 1: bin(7) = "111", which satisfies s(i) = s(i-1), an LFSR of length 1.
%e A397621 a(9) = 3: bin(9) = "1001". The shortest GF(2) LFSR recurrence satisfied by (1,0,0,1) has length 3 (connection polynomial 1+x+x^3 or similar of degree 3); no length-1 or length-2 LFSR reproduces this string, so a(9)=3.
%o A397621 (Python)
%o A397621 def a(n):
%o A397621     # Code after Peter Luschny optimizations
%o A397621     L, M = n.bit_length(), -1
%o A397621     B, C = 1, 1
%o A397621     R, S = 0, 0
%o A397621     for N in range(L):
%o A397621         bit = (n >> (L - 1 - N)) & 1
%o A397621         R = (R << 1) | bit
%o A397621         if (C & R).bit_count() & 1:
%o A397621             T = C
%o A397621             C ^= B << (N - M)
%o A397621             if (S << 1) <= N:
%o A397621                 S = N + 1 - S
%o A397621                 B = T
%o A397621                 M = N
%o A397621     return S
%Y A397621 Cf. A000120, A001317, A007088, A070939.
